<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0">
  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>
  <parent>
    <groupId>tech.mikhailov.ratchet</groupId>
    <artifactId>ratchet-parent</artifactId>
    <version>0.27.0</version>
  </parent>
  <artifactId>ratchet-tools</artifactId>
  <packaging>jar</packaging>
  <name>ratchet-tools</name>
  <description>The tools an agent actually reaches for: bash, read, write, edit, background jobs and
    a todo list. Opt-in, and the JDK only.</description>
  <!-- A THIRD MODULE, AND WHY IT IS SEPARATE RATHER THAN PART OF ratchet-llm.

       `Tool` is three strings and this project shipped no implementations, deliberately: the loop
       must not decide what a caller's agent can do. That principle is about the LOOP, and it stayed
       true while costing every consumer the same five hundred lines. One carried an entire client
       library — and therefore its loop, and therefore its transport — because four file tools came
       from it and were shaped by it. Writing them onto `Tool`/`Calling` took 505 lines and removed
       1,053.

       So the tools are here and not there. A consumer who wants them takes this dependency; one who
       does not is unaffected, and `Asking` still has no opinion about what it is handed. That is the
       same shape as `Retry.local()`: a shipped default nobody is obliged to use.

       THE SET IS MEASURED RATHER THAN GUESSED. See TOOLS.md at the root: 816 calls across six runs
       of one real task, where five tools carry 97%. -->
  <dependencies>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>tech.mikhailov.ratchet</groupId>
      <artifactId>ratchet-llm</artifactId>
    </dependency>
  </dependencies>
</project>
